#!/usr/bin/env bash
# fleet-escalation-drain — bounded-file maintainer for the escalation channel
# (fleet-ops#2677 + #2773).
#
# The escalation layer (NISH-ESCALATIONS.md + alert-repair packets) is
# append-only and accumulation is structural: stop-escalation-dispatch writes
# one NISH line per CAP-REACHED, KILL-ESCALATION, or LADDER-WALLED trip; the
# Alertmanager webhook writes one packet per firing alert. Without a drain
# the files grow monotonically and the boundary channel becomes unreadable
# (Nish can't scan 369 lines to find the one entry that needs a money call).
#
# Three drain operations, each idempotent and audit-friendly:
#
#   1. NISH-ESCALATIONS.md: archive RESOLVED / REVOKED-BY-PROBE lines to
#      nish-escalations-archive/<UTC-day>.md. nish-boundary-notify already
#      writes those markers when a page is delivered or probed-suppressed.
#      Non-class lines (legacy noise from pre-#1534 writes: LADDER-WALLED,
#      SEAT-UNHEALTHY, PI-DISPATCH-FAILED, ad-hoc heartbeat:/BOUNDARY:/
#      straitly/etc.) are also archived — the class-gate refuses new writes
#      of these classes, so anything left over is pre-gate history. After
#      the unconditional archive, if the live file is still over
#      FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_MAX_LINES (default 50), archive delivered
#      boundary entries (entries whose hash is in the seen set). Active
#      entries (NOT in the seen set) stay so a not-yet-delivered page is
#      never lost.
#
#   1b. alert-repair stuck-packet burst closure (fleet-ops#5647, converged
#      by fleet-ops#5622): the same run a stuck burst is detected, the
#      drain FILES one aggregate [stuck-packet] escalation issue, then
#      ARCHIVES every stuck packet under $PACKET_DIR/archived/stuck/
#      (never silently deleted — the file moves and a DISPOSITION decision
#      line naming the filed issue lands in actions.log) so the hourly
#      LOUD line cannot repeat forever for a settled burst. A NEW stuck
#      packet newer than the filed burst's watermark starts a fresh
#      filing; when filing is unavailable the run fails open (packets
#      stay, LOUD line remains).
#
#   2. alert-repair packets: delete packet-*.md files whose dispatch cycle
#      TERMINATED. The termination proof is chains.terminated.jsonl — the
#      ledger fleet-completion-canary appends to (with the mirror under
#      agent-state/alert-repair/) when a chain reaches a legal terminal
#      (green / detector-red / escalated). Each record carries the
#      alertname and end_ts; a packet with webhook name
#      packet-<alert>-<ts>.md is CONSUMED when a terminal record exists for
#      that alertname whose end_ts is >= the packet's dispatch instant
#      (the canary closes one cycle per alertname and absorbs every
#      intermediate re-dispatch of that episode under the last terminal).
#      A packet created AFTER the latest terminal (a fresh re-fire) or for
#      an alertname with NO terminal record is left in place — that is an
#      in-flight or genuinely-stuck chain, which belongs to the completion
#      canary's stall budget and escalation ladder, NOT to a silent drain.
#      actions.log is deliberately NOT used as a proof source: it is
#      bounded (rotated) so old packets' chains are unprovable there; the
#      ledger is append-only and complete.
#
#   3. Skip-list: never delete packet files without a `<alert>-<ts>.md`
#      timestamp suffix. The canary / guard scaffolding packets
#      (packet-11-completion-canary.md, packet-13-undersaturation-guard.md,
#      packet-17-organ-absence-rules.md, packet-red-main-2.md) have NO
#      webhook timestamp — they are owned by fleet-ops internals; draining
#      them would break the chain the canary is proving.
#
#   4. Bound-breach second stage (fleet-ops#5624): when the live file stays
#      over MAX_LINES for > FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_OVER_BOUND_AGE_S
#      (default 24h) — the state the unconditional + condition drains
#      cannot converge — a LOUD [BOUND-BREACH] alert fires once per breach
#      episode and the oldest live units (prose sections + delivered
#      formal entries) are digested into
#      $DIGEST_DIR/digest-YYYY-MM-DD.md until the file fits the bound.
#      Active (never-delivered) boundary entries are never digest
#      candidates; every removal is a logged disposition, never a silent
#      drop.
#
# Anti-recursion: this drain is a maintenance script, NOT an organ (no
# heartbeat metric). It does not create units, does not appear in
# config/fleet-organs.json, and does not need an absent() rule. The
# systemd timer that runs it inherits service.d/10-escalate.conf; a
# genuine fail-loud exit (a hard pre-condition failed) climbs the ladder
# to the senior auditor. A clean run with nothing to do is rc=0 silent.
#
# Environment seams (tests):
#   FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_AGENT_STATE    default /home/nish/workspaces/agent-state
#   FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_MAX_LINES      default 50
#   FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_TERMINATED     ledger path (default $PACKET_DIR/chains.terminated.jsonl)
#   FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_STUCK_AGE_S     webhook packet age past which a no-terminal
#                                          packet is LOUD-flagged then archived to
#                                          $PACKET_DIR/archived/stuck/ with a DISPOSITION
#                                          line in $PACKET_DIR/actions.log (fleet-ops#5647)
#                                          packet is flagged LOUD (default 21600 = 6h,
#                                          fleet-ops#2677: a packet older than ~6h
#                                          must be either consumed (terminal record)
#                                          or escalated loud so a never-terminating
#                                          chain cannot hide for a day)
#   FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_CLASSES        regex (must match nish-boundary-notify)
#   FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_DRY_RUN        1 = never modify files, only report
#   FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_OVER_BOUND_STAMP  breach stamp path
#   FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_OVER_BOUND_AGE_S  over-bound age before alert+digest
#                                          (default 86400 = 24h)
#   FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_DIGEST_DIR     digest output dir (default $ARCHIVE_DIR)
#   FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_BOUND_DIGEST   0 = alert only, never digest
#   FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_GH             gh binary (default gh; test stub seam)
#   FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_ISSUE_FILE     fleet-issue-file binary used to file
#                                         the stuck-packet escalation (default:
#                                         sibling bin/fleet-issue-file of this
#                                         script; test stub seam)
#   FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_ESCALATION_REPO  repo the escalation issue is filed
#                                         to (default Nishfleet/fleet-ops)
#
# Exit:
#   0  drain complete (idempotent: re-runs do nothing once the file is bounded)
#   1  hard pre-condition failed (jq missing, etc.) — climbs the ladder
#   2  usage error

set -euo pipefail

AGENT_STATE="${FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_AGENT_STATE:-/home/nish/workspaces/agent-state}"
NISH="${FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_NISH:-$AGENT_STATE/NISH-ESCALATIONS.md}"
ARCHIVE_DIR="${FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_ARCHIVE_DIR:-$AGENT_STATE/nish-escalations-archive}"
SEEN_FILE="${FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_SEEN:-$AGENT_STATE/lanes/nish-boundary-notify.seen}"
PACKET_DIR="${FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_PACKET_DIR:-$AGENT_STATE/alert-repair}"
TERMINATED="${FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_TERMINATED:-$PACKET_DIR/chains.terminated.jsonl}"
MAX_LINES="${FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_MAX_LINES:-50}"
STUCK_AGE_S="${FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_STUCK_AGE_S:-21600}"
CLASSES="${FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_CLASSES:-MONEY-BOUNDARY|LEGAL-BOUNDARY|PRODUCT-DIRECTION|CUSTOMER-DATA|CREDENTIAL-BOUNDARY|ONE-SHOT-PUBLIC-ACTION}"
DRY_RUN="${FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_DRY_RUN:-0}"
# Condition-resolution pass (fleet-ops#3996): ages out money-wall entries
# whose underlying provider condition has resolved. A provider is "walled"
# if ANY seat file under SEAT_DIR has http_status 402 or health_class
# quota_exhausted for that provider. A money-wall entry mentioning provider(s)
# is archived when ALL mentioned providers are no longer walled.
SEAT_DIR="${FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_SEAT_DIR:-$AGENT_STATE/lanes/seats}"
CONDITION_DRAIN="${FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_CONDITION_DRAIN:-1}"
# Provider names to scan for in entry text (lowercase, pipe-delimited regex).
# Sourced from the seat-file provider prefixes; extended with common aliases.
PROVIDER_NAMES="${FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_PROVIDER_NAMES:-openrouter|straitly|commandcode|bai|cline|poolside|groq|minimax|ollama|opencode|cursor|hetzner|mergegateway|orcarouter|xai-oauth|xkiro|zenmux}"
# Money-wall keywords that mark an entry as a provider money wall (not just
# any entry that happens to mention a provider name).
MONEY_KEYWORDS="${FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_MONEY_KEYWORDS:-402|quota_exhausted|credits exhausted|credit balance|insufficient_user_quota|out of credits|billing wall|quota wall|payment wall}"
# Bound-breach second stage (fleet-ops#5624). The stamp records when the
# live file was FIRST seen over MAX_LINES; it is removed the moment the
# file returns to bound, so only a persistent breach accumulates age.
OVER_BOUND_STAMP="${FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_OVER_BOUND_STAMP:-$AGENT_STATE/lanes/fleet-escalation-drain.over-bound}"
OVER_BOUND_AGE_S="${FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_OVER_BOUND_AGE_S:-86400}"
DIGEST_DIR="${FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_DIGEST_DIR:-$ARCHIVE_DIR}"
BOUND_DIGEST="${FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_BOUND_DIGEST:-1}"
# fleet-ops#5622: stuck-packet escalation wiring — one aggregate filing per
# stuck burst; every disposed packet is archived + decision-logged. Env
# seams keep the whole path hermetic for tests.
GH_BIN="${FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_GH:-gh}"
ESCALATION_REPO="${FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_ESCALATION_REPO:-Nishfleet/fleet-ops}"
ISSUE_FILE_BIN="${FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_ISSUE_FILE:-}"
if [[ -z "$ISSUE_FILE_BIN" ]]; then
    _here="$(cd "$(dirname "$(readlink -f "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")")" && pwd)"
    ISSUE_FILE_BIN="$_here/fleet-issue-file"
fi
STUCK_ARCH_DIR="${FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_STUCK_ARCH_DIR:-$PACKET_DIR/archived/stuck}"
STATE_FILE="${FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_STUCK_STATE:-$PACKET_DIR/stuck-escalation-state.json}"
ACTIONS_LOG="${FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_ACTIONS_LOG:-$PACKET_DIR/actions.log}"

log() {
    printf '[%s] [fleet-escalation-drain] %s\n' \
        "$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)" "$*" >&2
}

usage() {
    cat <<'EOF' >&2
usage: fleet-escalation-drain [--dry-run]

  --dry-run   report what would be archived/deleted; touch nothing
EOF
    exit 2
}

case "${1:-}" in
    "") ;;
    --dry-run) DRY_RUN=1 ;;
    -h|--help) usage ;;
    *) echo "fleet-escalation-drain: unknown arg: $1" >&2; usage ;;
esac

command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "fleet-escalation-drain: jq required" >&2; exit 1; }

if [ ! -d "$AGENT_STATE" ]; then
    echo "fleet-escalation-drain: agent-state missing: $AGENT_STATE" >&2
    exit 1
fi

nish_archived=0
nish_bounded=0
nish_condition_resolved=0
nish_condition_live=0
nish_body_resolved=0
nish_digested=0
packet_deleted=0
packet_skipped=0
packet_archived=0

# Body-level RESOLVED marker regex (fleet-ops#3996). Entries whose header class
# is NOT literally RESOLVED/REVOKED-BY-PROBE can still carry a resolution marker
# in their continuation lines — e.g. `  RESOLVED 2026-08-27T06:07Z: ...` under a
# CREDENTIAL-BOUNDARY header, or `- **RESOLVED by orchestrator ...**` in a prose
# section. These are closed conditions the drain must age out. The regex matches
# RESOLVED followed by either ` by ` (prose attribution) or a 4-digit year
# (formal timestamp), avoiding false positives on the bare word in random prose.
# NOTE: the default cannot use ${var:-...} inline because the regex's own braces
# ({4}) would close the parameter expansion; assign the default separately.
BODY_RESOLVED_RE="${FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_BODY_RESOLVED_RE:-}"
if [ -z "$BODY_RESOLVED_RE" ]; then
    BODY_RESOLVED_RE='RESOLVED[[:space:]]+(by[[:space:]]|[0-9]{4}-)'
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. NISH-ESCALATIONS.md drain
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Strategy:
#   a. Read the file as lines.
#   b. Classify each line:
#        - header (lines 1..6) — keep verbatim
#        - entry line (starts with <UTC ISO8601>) — RESOLVED / REVOKED-by-probe
#          -> archive unconditionally
#        - class-gated boundary line: archive ONLY when file still over
#          MAX_LINES AFTER the unconditional archive AND the line's hash is
#          in the seen set (i.e. nish-boundary-notify already delivered it).
#          Lines NOT in the seen set stay (a not-yet-delivered page is never
#          lost).
#        - non-class line (legacy noise: LADDER-WALLED, SEAT-UNHEALTHY,
#          PI-DISPATCH-FAILED, ad-hoc heartbeat:, BOUNDARY:, straitly, etc.)
#          -> archive unconditionally (the class-gate post-#1534 refuses new
#          writes, so leftover is pre-gate history).
#        - continuation line (indented body) — follow the immediately-preceding
#          header's classification.
#   c. Append archived entries to <ARCHIVE_DIR>/YYYY-MM-DD.md (one file per
#      UTC day, append-only, never rotated; reviewer finds every archived
#      escalation on the day it was archived).
#   d. Atomic rewrite of the live file with only kept lines.
#
# Active-delivery detection (fleet-ops#1458): the seen file is the post-delivery
# ledger — its key is sha256 of the original entry line. A class boundary
# line whose key is NOT in the seen set is either "still pending" or
# "delivered before the seen file existed"; the conservative rule keeps the
# line either way (matches fleet-escalation-completion wall-marker semantics:
# a hash in NISH means the chain is on Nish's board and must stay).

drain_nish() {
    if [ ! -f "$NISH" ]; then
        log "NISH-ESCALATIONS.md missing at $NISH — nothing to drain"
        return 0
    fi

    mkdir -p "$ARCHIVE_DIR"

    # Load seen keys (one per line). Empty/missing = no deliveries on record.
    declare -A seen_keys=()
    if [ -f "$SEEN_FILE" ]; then
        while IFS= read -r k; do
            [ -n "$k" ] && seen_keys["$k"]=1
        done < "$SEEN_FILE"
    fi

    local stamp_day
    stamp_day=$(date -u +%Y-%m-%d)
    local archive_path="$ARCHIVE_DIR/$stamp_day.md"
    local tmp_nish tmp_archive
    tmp_nish=$(mktemp -p "$AGENT_STATE" .nish-drain.XXXXXX)
    tmp_archive=$(mktemp -p "$AGENT_STATE" .nish-archive.XXXXXX)

    # Parse into entries: each entry is a list of lines starting with a
    # UTC ISO8601 timestamp header, followed by indented continuation lines
    # until the next timestamp header or EOF. The first 6 lines are the
    # file-format header (kept verbatim regardless of class).
    #
    # Strategy:
    #   1. First pass: read line-by-line, group into entries, classify the
    #      header:
    #        - RESOLVED / REVOKED-BY-PROBE -> archive_lines (with continuations)
    #        - non-class line (LADDER-WALLED, SEAT-UNHEALTHY, ad-hoc heartbeat:/
    #          BOUNDARY:/straitly/etc.) -> archive_lines (with continuations)
    #        - class-gated boundary -> tentatively kept_lines; the second pass
    #          below may promote to bounded_archive if the live file is still
    #          over MAX_LINES AFTER the unconditional archive.
    #   2. Second pass: if kept_lines still > MAX_LINES, walk and move
    #      delivered boundary entries (header AND continuations) into
    #      bounded_archive. Active entries (header hash NOT in seen set)
    #      stay.
    local -a kept_lines=()
    local -a archive_lines=()
    local -a bounded_archive=()
    local -a current_entry=()
    local line_ind=0

    flush_entry() {
        # Append current_entry to the destination chosen by header_class.
        local fe_ln
        # Body-level RESOLVED promotion (fleet-ops#3996): a class-gated
        # boundary entry (CREDENTIAL-BOUNDARY, MONEY-BOUNDARY, etc.) whose
        # continuation lines carry a RESOLVED marker is a closed condition —
        # promote from keep to archive so the live file only holds open
        # boundaries. The header class stays RESOLVED/REVOKED-BY-PROBE for
        # the original unconditional-archive path; this catches the case
        # where the resolution is recorded in the body, not the header.
        if [ "$header_class" = "keep" ] && [ "${#current_entry[@]}" -gt 1 ]; then
            local body_text=""
            local bi
            for ((bi = 1; bi < ${#current_entry[@]}; bi++)); do
                body_text="$body_text"$'\n'"${current_entry[$bi]}"
            done
            if printf '%s' "$body_text" | grep -qiE "$BODY_RESOLVED_RE"; then
                header_class="archive"
                nish_body_resolved=$((nish_body_resolved + 1))
            fi
        fi
        case "$header_class" in
            archive)
                for fe_ln in "${current_entry[@]}"; do
                    archive_lines+=("$fe_ln")
                done
                nish_archived=$((nish_archived + 1))
                ;;
            keep)
                for fe_ln in "${current_entry[@]}"; do
                    kept_lines+=("$fe_ln")
                done
                ;;
            bounded)
                for fe_ln in "${current_entry[@]}"; do
                    bounded_archive+=("$fe_ln")
                done
                nish_bounded=$((nish_bounded + 1))
                ;;
        esac
        current_entry=()
        header_class=""
    }

    header_class=""
    while IFS= read -r line || [ -n "$line" ]; do
        line_ind=$((line_ind + 1))

        # File-format header section: keep verbatim (first 6 lines).
        if [ "$line_ind" -le 6 ]; then
            kept_lines+=("$line")
            continue
        fi

        # Entry header: starts with a UTC ISO8601 timestamp. Real entries
        # carry either `2026-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ` (formal) or `2026-MM-DDTHH:MMZ`
        # (compact — heartbeat-style boundary entries) followed by a space.
        if [[ "$line" =~ ^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}T[0-9]{2}(:[0-9]{2}){1,2}Z[[:space:]] ]]; then
            # Flush any in-progress entry first.
            if [ "${#current_entry[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
                flush_entry
            fi
            current_entry=("$line")
            local class
            class=$(printf '%s' "$line" | awk '{print $2}')
            case "$class" in
                RESOLVED|REVOKED-BY-PROBE)
                    header_class="archive"
                    continue
                    ;;
            esac
            if printf '%s\n' "$CLASSES" | tr '|' '\n' | grep -qxF "$class"; then
                header_class="keep"  # tentative — second pass may promote to bounded
                continue
            fi
            header_class="archive"
            continue
        fi

        # Prose section header (`## ...`): flush the current formal entry so
        # its continuation lines do not absorb the prose section. The prose
        # section itself is kept here as a stray line — drain_nish_conditions
        # handles prose-section condition/body-RESOLVED drain in a separate
        # pass. Without this boundary, a formal entry with a body-RESOLVED
        # marker would archive every prose section below it (fleet-ops#3996).
        if [[ "$line" =~ ^##[[:space:]] ]]; then
            if [ "${#current_entry[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
                flush_entry
            fi
            kept_lines+=("$line")
            continue
        fi

        # Continuation / blank / other. Belongs to current entry.
        if [ "${#current_entry[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
            current_entry+=("$line")
        else
            # Stray continuation before any entry — keep verbatim (rare).
            kept_lines+=("$line")
        fi
    done < "$NISH"
    # Flush the last entry.
    if [ "${#current_entry[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
        flush_entry
    fi

    # Second pass: if the live file is still over MAX_LINES, fold the
    # delivered boundary entries into the archive. Active (undelivered)
    # boundary entries stay because their hash is not in the seen set.
    # We re-parse kept_lines into entries and promote headers whose hash
    # is in the seen set (with their continuations) into bounded_archive.
    local kept_count=${#kept_lines[@]}
    if [ "$kept_count" -gt "$MAX_LINES" ]; then
        local -a new_kept=()
        local -a pending_entry=()
        local pending_class=""
        flush_pending() {
            case "$pending_class" in
                bounded)
                    local fe_ln
                    for fe_ln in "${pending_entry[@]}"; do
                        bounded_archive+=("$fe_ln")
                    done
                    nish_bounded=$((nish_bounded + 1))
                    ;;
                *)
                    local fe_ln
                    for fe_ln in "${pending_entry[@]}"; do
                        new_kept+=("$fe_ln")
                    done
                    ;;
            esac
            pending_entry=()
            pending_class=""
        }
        local ln_ind=0
        for ln in "${kept_lines[@]}"; do
            ln_ind=$((ln_ind + 1))
            # Preserve the file-format header (first 6 lines of kept_lines).
            if [ "$ln_ind" -le 6 ]; then
                new_kept+=("$ln")
                continue
            fi
            if [[ "$ln" =~ ^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}T[0-9]{2}(:[0-9]{2}){1,2}Z[[:space:]] ]]; then
                if [ "${#pending_entry[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
                    flush_pending
                fi
                pending_entry=("$ln")
                local key
                key=$(printf '%s' "$ln" | sha256sum | cut -c1-32)
                if [ "${seen_keys[$key]:-0}" = "1" ]; then
                    pending_class="bounded"
                else
                    pending_class="keep"
                fi
                continue
            fi
            if [ "${#pending_entry[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
                pending_entry+=("$ln")
            else
                new_kept+=("$ln")
            fi
        done
        if [ "${#pending_entry[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
            flush_pending
        fi
        kept_lines=("${new_kept[@]}")
        log "live file still $kept_count lines (> MAX_LINES=$MAX_LINES); promoted $nish_bounded delivered boundary entries to archive"
        kept_count=${#kept_lines[@]}
    fi

    if [ "$nish_archived" -eq 0 ] && [ "$nish_bounded" -eq 0 ]; then
        rm -f "$tmp_nish" "$tmp_archive"
        log "NISH-ESCALATIONS.md: nothing to archive (file=$kept_count lines, max=$MAX_LINES)"
        return 0
    fi

    if [ "$DRY_RUN" = "1" ]; then
        log "DRY: would archive $nish_archived unconditional + $nish_bounded delivered-boundary entries; rewrite $kept_count-line live file (was $(wc -l < "$NISH"))"
        rm -f "$tmp_nish" "$tmp_archive"
        return 0
    fi

    local old_lines
    old_lines=$(wc -l < "$NISH")

    # Live file rewrite (kept only).
    {
        for ln in "${kept_lines[@]}"; do
            printf '%s\n' "$ln"
        done
    } > "$tmp_nish"
    # Atomic rename (same dir as live file).
    mv "$tmp_nish" "$NISH"

    # Archive append: header if first-of-day, then this batch's lines.
    {
        if [ ! -s "$archive_path" ]; then
            printf '# NISH-ESCALATIONS archive — %s (UTC)\n' "$stamp_day"
            printf '# Archived by fleet-escalation-drain (fleet-ops#2677).\n'
            printf '# Entries moved here are RESOLVED, REVOKED-BY-PROBE, non-class\n'
            printf '# legacy noise, or delivered boundary entries past the live-file\n'
            printf '# line cap. NEVER rotated; one file per UTC day.\n'
            printf '\n'
        fi
        for ln in "${archive_lines[@]}"; do
            printf '%s\n' "$ln"
        done
        for ln in "${bounded_archive[@]}"; do
            printf '%s\n' "$ln"
        done
    } > "$tmp_archive"
    cat "$tmp_archive" >> "$archive_path"
    rm -f "$tmp_archive"

    local new_lines
    new_lines=$(wc -l < "$NISH")
    log "NISH-ESCALATIONS.md: archived=$nish_archived bounded=$nish_bounded live_lines=$new_lines (was $old_lines) archive=$archive_path"
    return 0
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1b. NISH-ESCALATIONS.md condition-resolution pass (fleet-ops#3996)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The unconditional drain above archives RESOLVED / REVOKED / non-class noise
# and delivered boundary entries past MAX_LINES. But money-wall entries —
# both formal MONEY-BOUNDARY lines and prose `## date — title` sections the
# senior auditor writes about provider 402s — stay forever because they
# carry no RESOLVED marker even when the underlying provider condition has
# resolved (credits topped up, quota reset, seat came back). A ledger with
# no drain for these cannot distinguish live boundaries from closed ones.
#
# This pass:
#   1. Builds a provider health map from seat files under SEAT_DIR: a
#      provider is WALLED if ANY seat file for it has http_status 402 or
#      health_class quota_exhausted; otherwise it is HEALTHY (or ABSENT —
#      no seat file at all, treated as not-walled so a stale entry about a
#      provider whose seats were deleted does not linger).
#   2. Parses the live NISH-ESCALATIONS.md into blocks: the file-format
#      header (kept verbatim), formal timestamp entries (kept — the
#      unconditional drain already handled RESOLVED/REVOKED/non-class), and
#      prose sections (`## ...` headers with their body until the next `## `
#      or formal timestamp entry or EOF).
#   3. For each prose section AND each formal MONEY-BOUNDARY entry: scan the
#      block text for provider names (PROVIDER_NAMES) AND money-wall
#      keywords (MONEY_KEYWORDS). If both are present, the block is a
#      money-wall entry. If ALL providers it mentions are no longer walled,
#      the condition has RESOLVED -> archive. If any mentioned provider is
#      still walled, the condition is LIVE -> keep and count.
#   4. Non-money-wall blocks (no provider + money keyword) are kept
#      untouched — this pass never touches a credential, legal, or
#      product-direction boundary.
#
# Conservative by design: a money-wall entry is archived ONLY when every
# provider it names has zero walled seats. A single 402 seat for any
# mentioned provider keeps the entry live. This never silently drops a
# boundary — the proof is the seat health map, which is the same source
# pick_seat and the comeback-release path use.
#
# Environment seams (tests):
#   FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_SEAT_DIR       seat files directory
#   FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_CONDITION_DRAIN  0 = skip this pass entirely
#   FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_PROVIDER_NAMES  provider regex
#   FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_MONEY_KEYWORDS  money-wall keyword regex

build_provider_walled_map() {
    # Prints lines: <provider>\t<walled:0|1> for each provider that has at
    # least one seat file. A provider is walled if ANY seat has 402 or
    # quota_exhausted. Absent providers are implicitly not-walled (caller
    # treats a missing key as 0).
    if [ ! -d "$SEAT_DIR" ]; then
        return 0
    fi
    local f
    for f in "$SEAT_DIR"/*.json; do
        [ -e "$f" ] || continue
        local base prov
        base=$(basename "$f")
        prov="${base%%__*}"
        [ -n "$prov" ] || continue
        # Skip spawn-bench files — they are not health probes.
        [[ "$base" == *.spawn-bench.json ]] && continue
        local status hclass
        status=$(jq -r '.http_status // empty' "$f" 2>/dev/null || true)
        hclass=$(jq -r '.health_class // empty' "$f" 2>/dev/null || true)
        local walled=0
        if [ "$status" = "402" ] || [ "$hclass" = "quota_exhausted" ]; then
            walled=1
        fi
        # Aggregate: a provider is walled if ANY seat is walled.
        printf '%s\t%s\n' "$prov" "$walled"
    done | awk -F'\t' '{ if ($2==1) w[$1]=1; else if (!(($1) in w)) w[$1]=0 } END { for (k in w) printf "%s\t%d\n", k, w[k] }'
}

drain_nish_conditions() {
    if [ "$CONDITION_DRAIN" != "1" ]; then
        return 0
    fi
    if [ ! -f "$NISH" ]; then
        return 0
    fi

    # Build the provider walled map into an associative array.
    declare -A walled_map=()
    local p w
    while IFS=$'\t' read -r p w; do
        [ -n "$p" ] || continue
        walled_map["$p"]="$w"
    done < <(build_provider_walled_map)

    # Parse the live file into blocks. A block is either:
    #   - the file-format header (first 6 lines — kept verbatim)
    #   - a formal timestamp entry (starts with ISO timestamp — kept by this
    #     pass unless it is a MONEY-BOUNDARY with resolved providers)
    #   - a prose section (starts with `## ` — candidate for condition drain)
    # We walk line by line, grouping into blocks, and classify each block.
    mkdir -p "$ARCHIVE_DIR"
    local stamp_day
    stamp_day=$(date -u +%Y-%m-%d)
    local archive_path="$ARCHIVE_DIR/$stamp_day.md"
    local tmp_nish tmp_archive
    tmp_nish=$(mktemp -p "$AGENT_STATE" .nish-cond.XXXXXX)
    tmp_archive=$(mktemp -p "$AGENT_STATE" .nish-cond-arch.XXXXXX)

    local -a kept_lines=()
    local -a archive_lines=()
    local -a current_block=()
    local block_type=""   # header | formal | prose | other
    local block_text=""
    local line_ind=0
    local cond_body_resolved=0

    flush_block() {
        if [ "${#current_block[@]}" -eq 0 ]; then
            return
        fi
        case "$block_type" in
            header|other)
                local ln
                for ln in "${current_block[@]}"; do kept_lines+=("$ln"); done
                ;;
            formal)
                # Formal entries were already handled by the unconditional
                # drain. But a MONEY-BOUNDARY formal entry whose providers
                # have resolved is aged out here too (the unconditional drain
                # only archives RESOLVED/REVOKED/non-class, not live
                # MONEY-BOUNDARY entries with resolved conditions).
                local is_money=0
                if printf '%s' "$block_text" | grep -qiE "MONEY-BOUNDARY"; then
                    is_money=1
                fi
                if [ "$is_money" = "1" ] && \
                   printf '%s' "$block_text" | grep -qiE "($MONEY_KEYWORDS)" && \
                   printf '%s' "$block_text" | grep -qiE "($PROVIDER_NAMES)"; then
                    if _block_providers_resolved "$block_text"; then
                        local ln
                        for ln in "${current_block[@]}"; do archive_lines+=("$ln"); done
                        nish_condition_resolved=$((nish_condition_resolved + 1))
                    else
                        local ln
                        for ln in "${current_block[@]}"; do kept_lines+=("$ln"); done
                        nish_condition_live=$((nish_condition_live + 1))
                    fi
                else
                    local ln
                    for ln in "${current_block[@]}"; do kept_lines+=("$ln"); done
                fi
                ;;
            prose)
                # Prose section: first check for a body-level RESOLVED marker
                # (fleet-ops#3996) — e.g. `- **RESOLVED by orchestrator ...**`.
                # A prose section that records its own resolution is a closed
                # condition regardless of money-wall status; archive it.
                if printf '%s' "$block_text" | grep -qiE "$BODY_RESOLVED_RE"; then
                    local ln
                    for ln in "${current_block[@]}"; do archive_lines+=("$ln"); done
                    nish_body_resolved=$((nish_body_resolved + 1))
                    cond_body_resolved=$((cond_body_resolved + 1))
                # Then check if it is a money-wall entry with resolved providers.
                elif printf '%s' "$block_text" | grep -qiE "($MONEY_KEYWORDS)" && \
                   printf '%s' "$block_text" | grep -qiE "($PROVIDER_NAMES)"; then
                    if _block_providers_resolved "$block_text"; then
                        local ln
                        for ln in "${current_block[@]}"; do archive_lines+=("$ln"); done
                        nish_condition_resolved=$((nish_condition_resolved + 1))
                    else
                        local ln
                        for ln in "${current_block[@]}"; do kept_lines+=("$ln"); done
                        nish_condition_live=$((nish_condition_live + 1))
                    fi
                else
                    local ln
                    for ln in "${current_block[@]}"; do kept_lines+=("$ln"); done
                fi
                ;;
        esac
        current_block=()
        block_type=""
        block_text=""
    }

    # Check if all providers mentioned in a block are no longer walled.
    _block_providers_resolved() {
        local text="$1"
        local prov
        # Extract provider names found in the text (lowercased).
        local found
        found=$(printf '%s' "$text" | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]' | \
                grep -oE "($PROVIDER_NAMES)" | sort -u)
        [ -n "$found" ] || return 1  # no provider mentioned -> not a money-wall
        local all_resolved=1
        while IFS= read -r prov; do
            [ -n "$prov" ] || continue
            local pw="${walled_map[$prov]:-0}"
            if [ "$pw" = "1" ]; then
                all_resolved=0
                break
            fi
        done <<< "$found"
        [ "$all_resolved" = "1" ]
    }

    while IFS= read -r line || [ -n "$line" ]; do
        line_ind=$((line_ind + 1))

        # File-format header: first 6 lines kept verbatim.
        if [ "$line_ind" -le 6 ]; then
            if [ "$block_type" != "header" ] && [ "${#current_block[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
                flush_block
            fi
            block_type="header"
            current_block+=("$line")
            block_text="$block_text"$'\n'"$line"
            continue
        fi

        # Prose section header: `## ...`
        if [[ "$line" =~ ^##[[:space:]] ]]; then
            if [ "${#current_block[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
                flush_block
            fi
            block_type="prose"
            current_block=("$line")
            block_text="$line"
            continue
        fi

        # Formal timestamp entry: starts with ISO timestamp + space.
        if [[ "$line" =~ ^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}T[0-9]{2}(:[0-9]{2}){1,2}Z[[:space:]] ]]; then
            if [ "${#current_block[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
                flush_block
            fi
            block_type="formal"
            current_block=("$line")
            block_text="$line"
            continue
        fi

        # Continuation line: belongs to the current block.
        if [ "${#current_block[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
            current_block+=("$line")
            block_text="$block_text"$'\n'"$line"
        else
            # Stray line before any block — keep verbatim.
            if [ "$block_type" != "other" ] && [ "${#current_block[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
                flush_block
            fi
            block_type="other"
            current_block=("$line")
            block_text="$line"
        fi
    done < "$NISH"
    if [ "${#current_block[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
        flush_block
    fi

    if [ "$nish_condition_resolved" -eq 0 ] && [ "$cond_body_resolved" -eq 0 ]; then
        rm -f "$tmp_nish" "$tmp_archive"
        if [ "$nish_condition_live" -gt 0 ]; then
            log "condition-drain: $nish_condition_live live money-wall entries (no resolved conditions to archive)"
        fi
        return 0
    fi

    if [ "$DRY_RUN" = "1" ]; then
        log "DRY: condition-drain would archive $nish_condition_resolved resolved money-wall + $cond_body_resolved body-resolved entries ($nish_condition_live still live)"
        rm -f "$tmp_nish" "$tmp_archive"
        return 0
    fi

    local old_lines
    old_lines=$(wc -l < "$NISH")

    # Live file rewrite (kept only).
    {
        for ln in "${kept_lines[@]}"; do
            printf '%s\n' "$ln"
        done
    } > "$tmp_nish"
    mv "$tmp_nish" "$NISH"

    # Archive append.
    {
        if [ ! -s "$archive_path" ]; then
            printf '# NISH-ESCALATIONS archive — %s (UTC)\n' "$stamp_day"
            printf '# Archived by fleet-escalation-drain condition-pass (fleet-ops#3996).\n'
            printf '# Entries moved here are money-wall entries whose underlying\n'
            printf '# provider condition resolved (no 402 / quota_exhausted seats).\n'
            printf '# NEVER rotated; one file per UTC day.\n'
            printf '\n'
        fi
        for ln in "${archive_lines[@]}"; do
            printf '%s\n' "$ln"
        done
    } > "$tmp_archive"
    cat "$tmp_archive" >> "$archive_path"
    rm -f "$tmp_archive"

    local new_lines
    new_lines=$(wc -l < "$NISH")
    log "condition-drain: resolved=$nish_condition_resolved body_resolved=$cond_body_resolved live=$nish_condition_live live_lines=$new_lines (was $old_lines) archive=$archive_path"
    return 0
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1c. NISH-ESCALATIONS.md bound-breach second stage (fleet-ops#5624)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The unconditional + condition drains above converge the live file when
# the content is resolvable. A file can still sit over MAX_LINES for days
# on content with no drain path — undelivered prose sections, live
# money-walls, Nish-decision backlog. Before this pass the drain printed
# the same "still 114 lines" line every hour for days: the bound was
# decorative and the breach itself was escalated nowhere — the permanent
# LOUD / alarm-fatigue shape fleet-ops#5624 flags.
#
# This pass gives the over-bound state a converging path and makes the
# breach a detected state:
#
#   1. Persist: $OVER_BOUND_STAMP records when the file was FIRST seen
#      over MAX_LINES. It is removed the moment the file returns to
#      bound, so a transient spike never accumulates age.
#   2. Alert: when the stamp age exceeds OVER_BOUND_AGE_S (default 24h) a
#      LOUD [BOUND-BREACH] line names the breach — a detected state, not
#      the repeated generic "still N lines" log line. It re-fires each
#      run while the breach persists (same convention as STUCK-PACKET).
#   3. Converge: units (formal timestamp entries + `## ` prose sections,
#      with their continuation lines) are moved OLDEST-FIRST into
#      $DIGEST_DIR/digest-YYYY-MM-DD.md until the live file fits
#      MAX_LINES. Every digested unit gets a logged `digested:` line —
#      a logged disposition, never a silent drop. A `## DRAIN-DIGEST`
#      pointer unit is appended to the live file so the board names
#      where the stale items went.
#   4. Never-lose invariant: an ACTIVE boundary entry (a formal class
#      entry whose hash is NOT in the seen set — a page not yet
#      delivered) is never a digest candidate. If only active entries
#      remain over the bound, the file stays over and the LOUD line
#      keeps firing: that is real backlog (the notifier is behind), not
#      a decorative bound.
#
# Environment seams (tests):
#   FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_OVER_BOUND_STAMP  breach stamp path
#   FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_OVER_BOUND_AGE_S  breach age before alert+digest
#   FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_DIGEST_DIR        digest output dir
#   FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_BOUND_DIGEST      0 = alert only, never digest
drain_nish_bound() {
    if [ ! -f "$NISH" ]; then
        return 0
    fi

    local total
    total=$(wc -l < "$NISH")

    # At/under bound: a converged file clears the breach stamp.
    if [ "$total" -le "$MAX_LINES" ]; then
        if [ -f "$OVER_BOUND_STAMP" ]; then
            if [ "$DRY_RUN" != "1" ]; then
                rm -f "$OVER_BOUND_STAMP"
            fi
            log "bound-breach cleared: live file $total lines (<= MAX_LINES=$MAX_LINES); stamp removed"
        fi
        return 0
    fi

    local now_epoch now_iso stamp_epoch="" stamp_iso=""
    now_epoch=$(date -u +%s)
    now_iso=$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)
    if [ -f "$OVER_BOUND_STAMP" ]; then
        stamp_epoch=$(head -n1 "$OVER_BOUND_STAMP" | tr -cd '0-9')
        stamp_iso=$(sed -n '2p' "$OVER_BOUND_STAMP" | tr -d '\n')
    fi
    if [ -z "$stamp_epoch" ]; then
        # First observed over-bound — or an unreadable stamp, which the
        # rewrite self-heals by restarting the clock.
        if [ "$DRY_RUN" != "1" ]; then
            mkdir -p "$(dirname "$OVER_BOUND_STAMP")"
            { printf '%s\n' "$now_epoch"; printf '%s\n' "$now_iso"; } > "$OVER_BOUND_STAMP"
        fi
        log "live file $total lines (> MAX_LINES=$MAX_LINES) — bound-breach clock started $now_iso; digest fires at +${OVER_BOUND_AGE_S}s"
        return 0
    fi
    [ -n "$stamp_iso" ] || stamp_iso="epoch=$stamp_epoch"

    local breach_age=$((now_epoch - stamp_epoch))
    if [ "$breach_age" -le "$OVER_BOUND_AGE_S" ]; then
        log "live file $total lines (> MAX_LINES=$MAX_LINES) — over-bound since $stamp_iso (${breach_age}s <= ${OVER_BOUND_AGE_S}s); no digest yet"
        return 0
    fi

    if [ "$BOUND_DIGEST" != "1" ]; then
        log "LOUD [BOUND-BREACH] NISH-ESCALATIONS.md over MAX_LINES=$MAX_LINES for ${breach_age}s (> ${OVER_BOUND_AGE_S}s, since $stamp_iso) — bound-digest disabled (BOUND_DIGEST=0); file stays $total lines"
        return 0
    fi

    # Load seen keys for the active-entry check (same ledger drain_nish
    # uses: sha256 of the entry header line, first 32 hex).
    declare -A seen_keys=()
    if [ -f "$SEEN_FILE" ]; then
        while IFS= read -r k; do
            [ -n "$k" ] && seen_keys["$k"]=1
        done < "$SEEN_FILE"
    fi

    # Parse into preamble + units. Preamble = the 6-line file-format
    # header plus every line before the first unit (the format-
    # documentation block). A unit starts at a formal timestamp entry or
    # a `## ` prose header and runs to the next unit start or EOF;
    # intervening lines are its continuation.
    local -a preamble=() body=() u_start=() u_head=() u_active=()
    local line_ind=0 seen_unit=0
    local line
    while IFS= read -r line || [ -n "$line" ]; do
        line_ind=$((line_ind + 1))
        if [ "$line_ind" -le 6 ]; then
            preamble+=("$line")
            continue
        fi
        if [[ "$line" =~ ^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}T[0-9]{2}(:[0-9]{2}){1,2}Z[[:space:]] ]] || \
           [[ "$line" =~ ^##[[:space:]] ]]; then
            seen_unit=1
            u_start+=("${#body[@]}")
            u_head+=("$line")
            local act=0
            if [[ "$line" =~ ^[0-9]{4}- ]]; then
                local class key
                class=$(printf '%s' "$line" | awk '{print $2}')
                if printf '%s\n' "$CLASSES" | tr '|' '\n' | grep -qxF "$class"; then
                    key=$(printf '%s' "$line" | sha256sum | cut -c1-32)
                    if [ "${seen_keys[$key]:-0}" != "1" ]; then
                        act=1
                    fi
                fi
            fi
            u_active+=("$act")
            body+=("$line")
            continue
        fi
        if [ "$seen_unit" = "0" ]; then
            preamble+=("$line")
        else
            body+=("$line")
        fi
    done < "$NISH"

    local n_units=${#u_start[@]}
    local pre_n=${#preamble[@]}
    # Lines available for kept units: MAX_LINES minus preamble, minus one
    # line reserved for the DRAIN-DIGEST pointer appended on a digest run.
    local budget=$((MAX_LINES - pre_n - 1))
    [ "$budget" -lt 0 ] && budget=0

    # Digest oldest-first until the surviving units fit the budget.
    # Active (never-delivered) boundary entries are skipped — they stay
    # on the board no matter how old.
    local -a u_digest=()
    local i ulen
    for ((i = 0; i < n_units; i++)); do u_digest+=(0); done
    local remaining=${#body[@]}
    for ((i = 0; i < n_units; i++)); do
        [ "$remaining" -le "$budget" ] && break
        [ "${u_active[$i]}" = "1" ] && continue
        if [ $((i + 1)) -lt "$n_units" ]; then
            ulen=$((u_start[i + 1] - u_start[i]))
        else
            ulen=$((${#body[@]} - u_start[i]))
        fi
        u_digest[i]=1
        remaining=$((remaining - ulen))
    done

    local n_digested=0
    for ((i = 0; i < n_units; i++)); do
        [ "${u_digest[$i]}" = "1" ] && n_digested=$((n_digested + 1))
    done

    local final_lines=$((pre_n + remaining + (n_digested > 0 ? 1 : 0)))
    if [ "$n_digested" -eq 0 ]; then
        log "LOUD [BOUND-BREACH] NISH-ESCALATIONS.md over MAX_LINES=$MAX_LINES for ${breach_age}s (> ${OVER_BOUND_AGE_S}s, since $stamp_iso) — cannot converge: only active (undelivered) boundary entries remain over the bound; file stays $total lines"
        return 0
    fi
    log "LOUD [BOUND-BREACH] NISH-ESCALATIONS.md over MAX_LINES=$MAX_LINES for ${breach_age}s (> ${OVER_BOUND_AGE_S}s, since $stamp_iso) — digesting $n_digested oldest live entries; $total -> $final_lines lines"

    if [ "$DRY_RUN" = "1" ]; then
        for ((i = 0; i < n_units; i++)); do
            [ "${u_digest[$i]}" = "1" ] && log "DRY: would digest: ${u_head[$i]}"
        done
        log "DRY: bound-digest would move $n_digested units to $DIGEST_DIR/digest-$(date -u +%Y-%m-%d).md and rewrite the live file at $final_lines lines"
        return 0
    fi

    # Split body into digested / kept, preserving file order inside each.
    local -a digest_lines=() kept_out=()
    local s e j
    for ((i = 0; i < n_units; i++)); do
        s=${u_start[$i]}
        if [ $((i + 1)) -lt "$n_units" ]; then e=${u_start[$((i + 1))]}; else e=${#body[@]}; fi
        for ((j = s; j < e; j++)); do
            if [ "${u_digest[$i]}" = "1" ]; then
                digest_lines+=("${body[$j]}")
            else
                kept_out+=("${body[$j]}")
            fi
        done
    done

    mkdir -p "$DIGEST_DIR"
    local stamp_day digest_path
    stamp_day=$(date -u +%Y-%m-%d)
    digest_path="$DIGEST_DIR/digest-$stamp_day.md"
    if [ ! -s "$digest_path" ]; then
        {
            printf '# NISH-ESCALATIONS bound-breach digest — %s (UTC)\n' "$stamp_day"
            printf '# Moved by fleet-escalation-drain bound-digest (fleet-ops#5624):\n'
            printf '# oldest live entries after the live file stayed > MAX_LINES for\n'
            printf '# >%ss. Disposition: digested — preserved here, off the live board.\n' "$OVER_BOUND_AGE_S"
            printf '# NEVER rotated; one file per UTC day.\n'
            printf '\n'
        } > "$digest_path"
    fi
    {
        for ln in "${digest_lines[@]}"; do
            printf '%s\n' "$ln"
        done
    } >> "$digest_path"

    local pointer="## DRAIN-DIGEST $now_iso — $n_digested stale entries moved to $digest_path (fleet-ops#5624 bound-breach)"
    local tmp_nish
    tmp_nish=$(mktemp -p "$AGENT_STATE" .nish-bound.XXXXXX)
    {
        for ln in "${preamble[@]}"; do printf '%s\n' "$ln"; done
        for ln in "${kept_out[@]}"; do printf '%s\n' "$ln"; done
        printf '%s\n' "$pointer"
    } > "$tmp_nish"
    mv "$tmp_nish" "$NISH"

    for ((i = 0; i < n_units; i++)); do
        [ "${u_digest[$i]}" = "1" ] && log "digested: ${u_head[$i]}"
    done
    nish_digested=$((nish_digested + n_digested))
    if [ "$final_lines" -le "$MAX_LINES" ]; then
        rm -f "$OVER_BOUND_STAMP"
        log "bound-digest: moved $n_digested oldest entries to $digest_path; live_lines=$final_lines (was $total); breach stamp cleared"
    else
        # Active entries kept the file over bound — the breach persists,
        # so the stamp (and the LOUD line) stays.
        log "bound-digest: moved $n_digested oldest entries to $digest_path; live_lines=$final_lines (was $total) — STILL over MAX_LINES=$MAX_LINES on active entries; breach stamp kept"
    fi
    return 0
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. alert-repair packet drain
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A packet is consumed when its dispatch cycle TERMINATED in
# chains.terminated.jsonl — the append-only ledger fleet-completion-canary
# writes (STATE copies into agent-state/alert-repair/ as the reviewer
# mirror) when a chain reaches green / detector-red / escalated. Each
# record carries alertname + end_ts; the canary closes one cycle per
# alertname and records the LAST dispatch unit of the episode, absorbing
# every intermediate re-dispatch under that final terminal.
#
# Rule: packet packet-<alert>-<ts>.md is CONSUMED iff a terminal record
# exists for <alert> with end_ts >= <ts>. (ISO strings compare
# lexicographically.) A packet created after the latest terminal (a fresh
# re-fire, chain still in flight) or for an alertname with NO terminal
# record is left in place (never silently deleted). But a WEBHOOK packet
# older than FLEET_ESCALATION_DRAIN_STUCK_AGE_S (default 6h, fleet-ops#2677)
# with no terminal record is a genuinely STUCK chain — the drain flags it
# LOUD on stderr so the hourly journal (inherited by the escalation
# ladder) makes the failure visible; the Prometheus/Alertmanager absent()
# pipeline (the #4146 replacement for the retired completion-canary
# stall ladder) escalates a stuck chain. A loud flag at 6h, never a
# silent delete.
#
# Skip-list: packet files without a `<alert>-<ts>.md` timestamp suffix are
# canary / guard scaffolding packets with no webhook dispatch (e.g.
# packet-11-completion-canary.md, packet-13-undersaturation-guard.md,
# packet-red-main-2.md) — their lifecycles are owned by other mechanisms.
drain_packets() {
    if [ ! -d "$PACKET_DIR" ]; then
        log "packet dir missing at $PACKET_DIR — nothing to drain"
        return 0
    fi
    if [ ! -f "$TERMINATED" ]; then
        log "chains.terminated.jsonl missing at $TERMINATED — skipping packet drain (cannot prove consumption)"
        return 0
    fi

    # Map alertname -> latest terminal end_ts. One jq pass over the ledger.
    declare -A terminated_end=()
    local talert tend
    while IFS=$'\t' read -r talert tend; do
        [ -n "$talert" ] || continue
        if [[ -z "${terminated_end[$talert]:-}" || "$tend" > "${terminated_end[$talert]}" ]]; then
            terminated_end["$talert"]="$tend"
        fi
    done < <(jq -rc 'select((.alertname // "") != "" and (.end_ts // "") != "") | [.alertname, .end_ts] | @tsv' "$TERMINATED" 2>/dev/null || true)

    local pkt
    local -a stuck=()
    local stuck_cutoff
    stuck_cutoff=$(date -u -d "$STUCK_AGE_S seconds ago" +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ 2>/dev/null || true)
    for pkt in "$PACKET_DIR"/packet-*.md; do
        [ -e "$pkt" ] || continue
        local name
        name=$(basename "$pkt")

        # Skip non-webhook scaffolding (packet-11-completion-canary.md,
        # packet-13-undersaturation-guard.md, packet-red-main-2.md, etc.).
        # Webhook packets carry `-<8-digit-date>T<6-digit-time>Z.md`.
        if ! [[ "$name" =~ ^packet-([A-Za-z0-9_]+)-([0-9]{8}T([0-9]{6}|[0-9]{4}))Z\.md$ ]]; then
            packet_skipped=$((packet_skipped + 1))
            continue
        fi
        local alert="${BASH_REMATCH[1]}"
        local ts="${BASH_REMATCH[2]}"
        # 4-digit-time packet names (senior-auditor re-dispatch naming,
        # e.g. packet-X-20260905T0401Z.md) have minute precision; seconds
        # unknown — compare at minute granularity by pinning :00.
        if [ ${#ts} -eq 13 ]; then ts="${ts}00"; fi

        # Packet dispatch instant as ISO (lexical compare vs ledger end_ts).
        local iso="${ts:0:4}-${ts:4:2}-${ts:6:2}T${ts:9:2}:${ts:11:2}:${ts:13:2}Z"
        local latest="${terminated_end[$alert]:-}"
        local consumed=0
        if [ -n "$latest" ] && { [[ "$iso" < "$latest" ]] || [[ "$iso" == "$latest" ]]; }; then
            consumed=1
        fi
        if [ "$consumed" = "1" ]; then
            if [ "$DRY_RUN" = "1" ]; then
                log "DRY: would delete $name (alert=$alert packet=$iso ledger_end=$latest)"
                packet_deleted=$((packet_deleted + 1))
                continue
            fi
            if rm -f "$pkt"; then
                packet_deleted=$((packet_deleted + 1))
                log "deleted $name (alert=$alert packet=$iso ledger_end=$latest)"
            else
                log "WARN: failed to delete $name (continuing)"
            fi
            continue
        fi
        packet_skipped=$((packet_skipped + 1))
        # Not consumed: in-flight re-fire (created after the latest terminal)
        # or never-terminated. Age-gate the LOUD flag so a fresh re-fire is
        # not noise.
        if [ -n "$stuck_cutoff" ] && [[ "$iso" < "$stuck_cutoff" ]]; then
            stuck+=("$name (packet=$iso)")
        fi
    done
    if [ "${#stuck[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
        escalate_stuck_packets "${stuck[@]}"
    fi
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# fleet-ops#5622: stuck-packet escalation + terminal disposition.
#
# A packet older than STUCK_AGE_S with NO terminated chain is a repair
# claim nobody picked up; re-listing it hourly is not convergence. Every
# stuck packet passed in gets a terminal disposition in the SAME run:
#
#   disposed -> the burst escalation issue was filed (or the state file's
#               newest-packet watermark shows an existing filing already
#               covers this packet); the packet is archived under
#               archived/stuck/ and a DISPOSITION decision line with
#               terminal=escalated-filed (or terminal=reasoned-drop when
#               the existing filing covers it) + the issue number is
#               appended to actions.log.
#   kept     -> the filing path failed (no fleet-issue-file, a non-zero
#               exit, or no issue number came back). Fail open exactly as
#               today: the packet stays in place and the LOUD line keeps
#               naming it — degraded but loud, identical to pre-#5622.
#
# The state file records the newest stuck packet instant covered by the
# last filing, so a fresh re-fire AFTER a filing starts a NEW burst
# instead of vanishing under the old one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
escalate_stuck_packets() {
    local -a stuck=("$@")
    local n_stuck=${#stuck[@]}

    # Stuck packets arrive in glob (oldest-first) order; the LAST entry
    # carries the newest dispatch instant.
    local newest_iso=""
    newest_iso="${stuck[$((n_stuck - 1))]##*packet=}"
    newest_iso="${newest_iso%\)}"

    local filed_issue=""
    if [ -f "$STATE_FILE" ] && command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        local state_max state_issue
        state_max="$(jq -r '.newest_packet_iso // ""' "$STATE_FILE" 2>/dev/null || true)"
        state_issue="$(jq -r '.issue // ""' "$STATE_FILE" 2>/dev/null || true)"
        if [ -n "$state_max" ] && [ -n "$state_issue" ] \
            && [[ "$newest_iso" < "$state_max" || "$newest_iso" == "$state_max" ]]; then
            filed_issue="$state_issue"
        fi
    fi

    local issue_no=""
    if [ "$DRY_RUN" = "1" ]; then
        issue_no=""
    elif [ -n "$filed_issue" ]; then
        issue_no="$filed_issue"
    else
        issue_no="$(file_stuck_escalation_issue "$n_stuck" "$newest_iso")" || issue_no=""
        if [ -n "$issue_no" ]; then
            printf '{"filed_ts": "%s", "newest_packet_iso": "%s", "issue": "%s", "n_packets": %d}\n' \
                "$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)" "$newest_iso" "$issue_no" "$n_stuck" > "$STATE_FILE"
        fi
    fi

    local pkt leftover=0
    for pkt in "${stuck[@]}"; do
        local name="${pkt%% (*}"
        if [ "$DRY_RUN" = "1" ]; then
            log "DRY: would archive stuck packet $name (escalated + decision-logged)"
            leftover=$((leftover + 1))
            continue
        fi
        if [ -z "$issue_no" ]; then
            leftover=$((leftover + 1))
            continue
        fi
        [ -f "$PACKET_DIR/$name" ] || continue
        mkdir -p "$STUCK_ARCH_DIR"
        if mv "$PACKET_DIR/$name" "$STUCK_ARCH_DIR/$name" 2>/dev/null; then
            local terminal="escalated-filed"
            [ -n "$filed_issue" ] && terminal="reasoned-drop"
            log_disposition "$name" "$terminal" "$issue_no"
            # fleet-ops#5647 contract kept through the #5622 rebase: the
            # summary reports same-run archives and the journal names each
            # archived packet, so the drain stays observable per packet.
            packet_archived=$((packet_archived + 1))
            log "archived $name terminal=$terminal issue=$issue_no (actions.log)"
            # The dispatch instant lives in the FILENAME, not the mtime; bump
            # the archived copy's mtime to the disposition instant so the
            # live-unclaimed metric (find packet-*.md -mmin +360) cannot
            # count a disposed packet as unclaimed forever.
            touch "$STUCK_ARCH_DIR/$name" 2>/dev/null || true
        else
            log "WARN: failed to archive $name — leaving in place (loud line will re-flag it)"
            leftover=$((leftover + 1))
        fi
    done

    # LOUD only names what a disposition run could NOT settle — when every
    # stuck packet was disposed the line goes silent and the signal has
    # converged instead of repeating hourly forever.
    if [ "$leftover" -gt 0 ]; then
        local s
        local head_line=""
        for s in "${stuck[@]:0:10}"; do
            head_line="$head_line $s"
        done
        log "LOUD [STUCK-PACKET] $n_stuck webhook packet(s) older than ${STUCK_AGE_S}s have NO terminated chain in $TERMINATED — escalation filing unavailable, packets kept (never silently delete); left for the Prometheus/Alertmanager absent() pipeline to escalate:$head_line"
    elif [ -n "$issue_no" ]; then
        log "stuck-packet escalation: $n_stuck packet(s) disposed -> issue #$issue_no (archived under $STUCK_ARCH_DIR)"
    elif [ "$DRY_RUN" = "1" ]; then
        log "DRY: $n_stuck stuck packet(s) would be escalated + archived"
    fi
}

log_disposition() {
    # One reasoned-decision line per disposed packet in actions.log (the
    # same append surface every other alert-repair actor uses).
    printf '[%s] DISPOSITION stuck-packet packet=%s terminal=%s issue=%s\n' \
        "$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)" "$1" "$2" "$3" \
        >> "$ACTIONS_LOG" 2>/dev/null || true
}

file_stuck_escalation_issue() {
    local n_packets="$1"
    local newest_iso="$2"
    local issue_file="$ISSUE_FILE_BIN"
    if [ ! -x "$issue_file" ]; then
        log "WARN fleet-issue-file missing/not executable at $issue_file — cannot file stuck-packet escalation"
        return 1
    fi
    local cutoff_iso
    cutoff_iso="$(date -u -d "$STUCK_AGE_S seconds ago" +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ 2>/dev/null || echo unknown)"
    local title="[stuck-packet] $n_packets alert-repair packet(s) older than ${STUCK_AGE_S}s with no terminated chain"
    local body
    body=$(cat <<EOF
metric: every firing alert packet in agent-state/alert-repair/ older than ${STUCK_AGE_S}s reaches a terminal disposition (terminated chain, filed repair claim, or reasoned drop with a logged decision); the unclaimed-packet count trends to 0, not up.

observed: the hourly escalation-drain found $n_packets webhook packet(s) with dispatch instants older than $cutoff_iso that have NO terminated chain in chains.terminated.jsonl — the terminal ledger (chains.terminated.jsonl, written by the retired fleet-completion-canary mirror) stopped recording terminals, so the drain's absent() fallback re-listed the same packets hourly and the backlog only grew. This issue is the drain's own bounded escalation for the current burst (fleet-ops#5622).

evidence:
- drain run at $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ): $n_packets stuck packet(s), newest dispatch instant $newest_iso
- terminal ledger: agent-state/alert-repair/chains.terminated.jsonl
- the packets are archived under agent-state/alert-repair/archived/stuck/ with a per-packet DISPOSITION decision line in actions.log (terminal=escalated-filed) — never silently deleted

accept:
- every packet in the dispositioned burst has a terminal disposition (chain, issue, or reasoned drop with a logged DISPOSITION line in actions.log).
- the underlying firing alerts are picked up by the normal alert-repair dispatch / intake repair-rung path; this issue is the escalation surface, not the repair worker itself.
- prevention mechanism (fleet-ops#366): the drain now files this escalation and archives + decision-logs the packets IN THE SAME RUN that detects them, so the hourly LOUD line cannot repeat forever for a settled burst; a NEW stuck packet after the filing starts a fresh filing.

termination: the burst is closed when every archived/stuck packet named by its DISPOSITION line has terminal=escalated-filed (or reasoned-drop) in actions.log and new dispatches resume producing terminal records in chains.terminated.jsonl; the drain's stuck set returns to 0 on the next hourly run.

Relates to #5622
EOF
)
    local out
    if ! out="$("$issue_file" file -R "$ESCALATION_REPO" --title "$title" --body "$body" 2>&1)"; then
        log "WARN stuck-packet filing failed (rc non-zero): $out"
        return 1
    fi
    local num
    num="$(printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -oE 'issues/[0-9]+' | grep -oE '[0-9]+$' | tail -1)"
    if [ -z "$num" ]; then
        num="$(printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -oE '#[0-9]+' | tail -1 | tr -d '#')"
    fi
    if [ -z "$num" ]; then
        log "WARN fleet-issue-file returned no issue number"
        return 1
    fi
    printf '%s' "$num"
}

drain_nish
drain_nish_conditions
drain_nish_bound
drain_packets

log "summary: nish_archived=$nish_archived nish_bounded=$nish_bounded nish_body_resolved=$nish_body_resolved nish_condition_resolved=$nish_condition_resolved nish_condition_live=$nish_condition_live nish_digested=$nish_digested packet_deleted=$packet_deleted packet_skipped=$packet_skipped packet_archived=$packet_archived dry_run=$DRY_RUN"
exit 0
